> Quickstart guide
__doc_meta__: true
description: 'Getting started with configatron'
<

> I'm a library developer, and I want to use Configatron for my configs.
    At a high level, the strategy is:
    ++  Define a configuration protocol within your library
    ++  Reference that protocol when accessing configs, either by the
        ``~MyConfigClass`` shorthand or by ``get_active_cfg``
    ++  Library users create a concrete implementation of your protocol
        class as an ``ext_dataclass`` using ``dcei``, and are responsible
        for configuring their own config backends
    ++  If they want to use their own configuration mechanisms, they can always
        use an instance of ``configatron.prebaked.backends.memory.MemoryBackend``
        as an adapter

    >
    __embed__: 'code/python'
        from typing import Protocol

        from configatron import CfgMeta
        from configatron import Secret


        class MyLibraryConfig(Protocol, metaclass=CfgMeta):
            host: str
            port: int
            app_secret: Secret[str]


        async def my_library_code(server):
            # Alternate spelling: ``config = get_active_cfg(MyLibraryConfig)``
            config = ~MyLibraryConfig

            async with server.bind([f'{config.host}:{config.port}']):
                # Concise inline references are supported
                await server.set_secret((~MyLibraryConfig).app_secret)

> I'm importing a library that uses configatron for its configs, and need to
> specify them.
    At a high level, the strategy is:
    ++  You define a concrete implementation of the abstract config defined
        within the library, as an ``ext_dataclass`` using ``dcei``
    ++  When starting up your application/script/etc, create a ``CfgManager``
        instance and pass it all of your concrete class implementations, as
        well as whatever backends you need to load the actual values
    ++  Configatron will handle the rest

    >
    __embed__: 'code/python'
        import anyio
        from configatron import CfgField
        from configatron import CfgManager
        from configatron import CfgSource
        from configatron import Configatron
        from configatron.prebaked.backends.env_vars import EnvVarBackend
        from configatron.prebaked.backends.tomlfile import TomlFileBackend
        from dcei import ext_dataclass
        from dcei import ext_field
        from my_library import MyLibraryConfig
        from my_library import my_library_code


        @ext_dataclass(Configatron(namespace='my.app'))
        class MyConfigImpl(MyLibraryConfig):
            # By default, we'll search all backends for the field name, ex
            # ``host`` and ``port``, respectively
            host: str
            port: int
            # Specifying the source backend is optional; only if you want to limit to
            # a specific (prioritized) list of backends, or have aliases for the field
            # names. By default we'll check all of them, and apply them in
            # precedence order from your CfgManager
            app_secret: str = ext_field(CfgField(
                CfgSource(
                    backend='env_vars',
                    # The env var backend interprets this as ``MY_APP_COOKIE_SECRET``,
                    # using the config class' namespace
                    lookup_keys=['COOKIE_SECRET'])))


        class MyServerObject:

            # During startup
            def on_app_start(self):
                cfg_manager = CfgManager(
                    MyConfigImpl,
                    backends={
                        'config_file': TomlFileBackend('my_config.toml'),
                        'env_vars': EnvVarBackend()})
                cfg_manager.load_once_sync()

                # The loaded config is now available to the application code that
                # requires it
                anyio.run(my_library_code, self)

    Alternatively, if you don't want to use configatron, you can instead use
    an instance of ``configatron.prebaked.backends.memory.MemoryBackend``
    as an adapter to whatever configuration mechanism you want.

> I'm an application developer, and I want to use configatron for my own
> configs.
    At a high level, the strategy is:
    ++  Define a config as both an abstract **and** concrete config class,
        using both the ``CfgMeta`` metaclass and as an ``ext_dataclass``
        using ``dcei``
    ++  Reference it when accessing configs, either by the
        ``~MyConfigClass`` shorthand or by ``get_active_cfg``
    ++  When starting up your application/script/etc, create a ``CfgManager``
        instance and pass it all of your concrete class implementations, as
        well as whatever backends you need to load the actual values
    >
    __embed__: 'code/python'
        import anyio
        from configatron import CfgField
        from configatron import CfgManager
        from configatron import CfgMeta
        from configatron import CfgSource
        from configatron import Configatron
        from configatron import Secret
        from configatron.prebaked.backends.env_vars import EnvVarBackend
        from configatron.prebaked.backends.tomlfile import TomlFileBackend
        from dcei import ext_dataclass
        from dcei import ext_field
        from my_library import MyLibraryConfig
        from my_library import my_library_code


        @ext_dataclass(Configatron(namespace='my.app'))
        class MyApplicationConfig(metaclass=CfgMeta):
            # By default, we'll search all backends for the field name, ex
            # ``host`` and ``port``, respectively
            host: str
            port: int
            # Specifying the source backend is optional; only if you want to limit to
            # a specific (prioritized) list of backends, or have aliases for the field
            # names. By default we'll check all of them, and apply them in
            # precedence order from your CfgManager
            app_secret: Secret[str] = ext_field(CfgField(
                CfgSource(
                    backend='env_vars',
                    # The env var backend interprets this as ``MY_APP_COOKIE_SECRET``,
                    # using the config class' namespace
                    lookup_keys=['COOKIE_SECRET'])))


        async def my_application_code(server):
            # Alternate spelling: ``config = get_active_cfg(MyApplicationConfig)``
            config = ~MyApplicationConfig

            async with server.bind([f'{config.host}:{config.port}']):
                # Concise inline references are supported
                await server.set_secret((~MyApplicationConfig).app_secret)


        class MyServerObject:

            # During startup
            def on_app_start(self):
                cfg_manager = CfgManager(
                    MyApplicationConfig,
                    backends={
                        'config_file': TomlFileBackend('my_config.toml'),
                        'env_vars': EnvVarBackend()})
                cfg_manager.load_once_sync()

                # The loaded config is now available to the application code that
                # requires it
                anyio.run(my_application_code, self)

> What about async?
    Configatron uses ``anyio`` for async support. As long as it's available
    (for example, by installing via ``pip install configatron[async]``),
    you'll be able to call ``cfg_manager.load_once_async()`` within a
    coroutine.

    Note that this also requires the backends you use to support async loading.
    **All prebaked backends ship with async support.**
